# Horo SDK compatibility and migration — edition 1

This guide ships with extension SDK **@HORO_EXTENSION_SDK_VERSION@**, engine
**@HORO_ENGINE_VERSION@**, source revision **@HORO_SOURCE_REVISION@**. Read it and
the sibling [machine-readable matrix](CompatibilityMatrix.json) directly from
an extracted SDK archive; no engine source checkout or network connection is
needed. The edition versions this document format, not the native ABI.

## Select an exact snapshot

The engine is in development. Several incompatible gameplay snapshots use the
same `0.1.0` engine/extension SDK version. A version string is not a release
attestation or a unique archive identity. Preserve the engine revision, SDK ZIP
SHA-256, platform, toolchain/configuration and generated descriptor revision.
`unknown` revision means provenance is unavailable, not verified compatibility.
An SDK built before this guide existed must be identified from its retained
archive/provenance; do not assign it the current snapshot's contracts.

| Contract in this archive | Version / admission |
|---|---|
| Engine and extension SDK | @HORO_ENGINE_VERSION@ / @HORO_EXTENSION_SDK_VERSION@; revision @HORO_SOURCE_REVISION@ |
| External native host ABI | `horo-extension` @HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAJOR_VERSION@.@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MIN_HOST_MINOR@ inclusive to @HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAX_HOST_MAJOR_EXCLUSIVE@.0 exclusive; declarations through @HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAJOR_VERSION@.@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_PUBLISHED_MINOR@ |
| Extension descriptor | `extension.json`, schema 1 |
| Package manifest | `horo-package.toml`, schema 1; owns package identity/dependencies |
| Gameplay C++ boundary in this engine snapshot | Native boundary 21 / `gameplay-sdk21`; separate exact-fingerprint build-tree SDK |
| Author CI platform profiles | Linux x64, macOS arm64, macOS x64, Windows x64 |

The external extension range does not imply that every table/capability works on
every host in that range. Require the minor and callbacks actually used. Platform
profiles are supported build/test routes, not a claim that release archives exist
or that this snapshot has passed every platform. The extension SDK has no engine
library link dependency; native libraries still require matching OS/architecture
and their own private dependency/toolchain compatibility. No Android, mobile,
cross-architecture, or generic C++ binary compatibility is promised here.

## External C ABI 1.0 → 1.1

The major stays 1. Load/unload entry points and the original host-table prefix
remain supported. The optional inert `horo_extension_query` negotiates minimum
host minor, required functions and table size before load. Missing query means
legacy minor 0, never an inferred modern profile. Modules needing negotiation
should add the query and also check requirements in load for older hosts.

Source migration: include the packaged `Horo/Extensions/ExtensionAbi.h` through
`HoroEngine::ExtensionSdk`. Status/setting wire tags use the public `uint32_t`
typedefs rather than compiler-sized enum tags; replace explicitly spelled old
enum tags with those typedef names and rebuild. Nonstandard short-enum binaries
are outside the compatibility promise. Never transfer STL, exceptions, allocators
or C++ deletion across this boundary. A non-null importer context needs its
module destroy callback; failed registration leaves ownership with the module.
After accepted registration, host-owned leases keep the originating code alive.

An existing binary using the original compliant C prefix does not need a rebuild
merely because the host minor increased. A changed source/wire representation or
new required callback does. Rollback: retain the old exact SDK/header and binary,
remove new-minor requirements/features, then validate against the older host;
do not relabel a new-minor binary as legacy.

## External C ABI 1.1 → 1.2 → 1.3

Minor 2 adds platform-service provider registration. Minor 3 adds the provider
operation lifecycle; neither changes the major or earlier host prefixes.
Version-1 provider descriptors must retain the original prefix size:
`offsetof(HoroPlatformServicesProviderDescriptor, operations)`. Replacing it with
new-header `sizeof` would advertise an unimplemented tail.

To use operations, require host minor 3, use provider descriptor version 2 and its
full size, and provide a non-null, exact-version-1 `HoroPlatformProviderOperations`
table. Rebuild that provider. Old factory-only providers can still load but the
operation host reports `platform.lifecycle.unsupported_profile`; loading is not
operation availability. Providers map completion codes to
`HoroPlatformProviderResultCode`, not private SDK codes; unknown values become
Unknown. This semantic fix does not change the completion layout. Failure
payloads must not contain private error text, credentials or account identifiers.

The module retains its native code/candidate and must close ingress and drain
before retire/destroy. BUSY is retried on the owner lane; cancellation is not proof
of drain. Rollback to a 1.2 host needs the old factory-only binary and original
descriptor prefix, with operation-dependent features disabled. Rollback to a 1.1
host also removes platform-provider contributions. Do not silently fall back to a
different provider or fabricate a successful service start.

## Gameplay SDK8 → SDK9 (native boundary 8 → 9)

This is a **breaking C++ layout/vtable and fingerprint change**, not extension
host ABI 1.4. `GameRuntimeContext` gains an injected Physics context and the
behavior backend gains its accessor. Rebuild every native gameplay module and
generated descriptor bundle with the same engine, compiler, platform and
configuration. Old boundaries/fingerprints are rejected before factory entry.
The separate gameplay SDK remains a build-tree export, not a relocatable installed
SDK supplied by this archive. This guide needs no checkout to read; producing a
gameplay module still needs that separate matching SDK.

Replacement access: include `Horo/Gameplay/GameplayPhysicsContext.h` through
`HoroEngine::GameplayPhysicsIntegration` and opt into the `Physics` component of
`HoroEngineGameplay`. Acquire the injected holder with its exact module, Scene ID
and activation generation. It returns `PhysicsQueryEventCapability`, not a world
pointer/native solver. Missing/denied context stays explicitly unavailable;
existing loader call expressions do not implicitly activate Physics. Lua's private
`ctx.physics.acquire()` returns a capability or `nil, {domain, code}`. Editor Play
Physics composition remains unavailable in this snapshot; SDK9 does not promise
editor Physics activation. Native Start/Stop, behavior/Lua adapters, headless
composition and external module projects must agree on the new boundary.

Rollback: stop the host and restore the complete SDK8 engine, matching module
binaries and descriptor bundles, project backup and locks. Rebuilding SDK9 source
against SDK8 requires removing the Physics-context/accessor use and its component
link; never load an SDK9 module into SDK8 by changing a manifest/fingerprint.

## Retirement source API migration in this snapshot

Host integrations use `ExtensionManager::RetireExtension`, inspect its attributed
retirement report, and call `FinalizeRetirements` only after admitted work has drained
on the owner lane. Registries can attach the typed retirement owner to actual
callback/job/resource/UI/service publications. Manager move operations were
removed: own it at a stable address (for example, `std::unique_ptr`) instead of
move-assigning into a live manager. This is a host C++ source change; the external
C ABI entry-point version is unchanged. Rebuild host consumers using that API.

Retirement withdraws publications and rejects later retained-snapshot calls while
admitted work pins provider/dependency code. BUSY, off-lane or surviving-work
teardown can report `RestartRequired` and retain native code for the process
lifetime; this is deliberately not leak-free unload or `Complete`. The manager's
1024-entry bound is per manager, not globally bounded across failed recreation.
Disable/update/remove remain restart-default. Rollback requires a stopped host
and the previous coherent host/package set; never force-unload retained code.

## Offline upgrade and rollback checklist

1. Retain the previous engine artifact/revision, SDK ZIP/digest, package binaries,
   lock, configuration and project backup outside the candidate staging area.
2. Read the candidate archive's matrix and this version-specific guide. Compare
   major/minor/table requirements, schema, platform and gameplay fingerprint.
3. Update the author project's SDK lock with exact archive URLs and SHA-256 for
   each selected platform. An archive already retained locally can be inspected
   offline; the supplied CI bootstrap downloads pinned archives and is not an
   offline restore tool. No placeholder digest is a release attestation.
4. Rebuild only the affected contracts described above, then run the packaged
   manifest validator and trusted-native conformance harness before packaging.
   They do not grant trust or prove full package lifecycle activation.
5. Stop the old host, stage the coherent candidate and obtain explicit local
   trust approval for its exact identity. Restart and check real functionality.
6. On failure, stop again, restore the retained coherent set and project backup,
   re-evaluate trust and restart. Preserve failure diagnostics. This is a manual
   safe rollback procedure, not an invented transactional package-upgrade API.

Do not infer compatibility from “latest”, load order, successful compilation,
archive integrity alone, or a version string shared by development snapshots.

## External C ABI 1.3 → 1.4 editor activities

Minor 4 appends `registerEditorActivity`; the 1.3 host prefix ends at
`offsetof(HoroExtensionHostApi, registerEditorActivity)`. Old modules retain their
prefix sizes and negotiate independently. Activity modules require minor 4 and
`HORO_EXTENSION_REQUIRES_EDITOR_ACTIVITY`, include `EditorActivityAbi.h`, declare
exact `editor.activity_item` and `editor.panel` claims and require the host
`editor.activity` capability. Headless and uncomposed editor hosts reject the
request explicitly. The generic conformance tool has no editor publication host;
its rejection of this required profile is expected and does not prove GUI support.

Session version 1 advertises a finite node mask for standard text, label, action,
stack and group form nodes. Other standard controls need a later explicitly
negotiated transport. A package never supplies a draw callback or renderer handle.
Snapshots strictly increase revision and are copied synchronously on the owner
lane. Semantic actions run through the application job system with cancellation
and module-code leases; their borrowed result sink is valid only during that call.
Discard the session before module unload. Package icon resources use the bounded
static SVG profile documented in the engine migration guide.

The shared activity header keeps its C11 typedef spellings and exposes the same
struct names directly to C++ consumers; its action callback uses an equivalent
C++ type alias. These declaration-only differences do not alter transport layout,
numeric constants, callback signatures, or version negotiation. No package data
migration is required. Both the strict C11 consumer and C++ public-header consumer
check the append-only host prefix and callback contracts; the relocated installed
SDK consumer remains the packaging compatibility check.

## Gameplay SDK9 → SDK10 (native boundary 9 → 10)

Rebuild native gameplay modules and generated descriptor bundles against the exact
engine/toolchain fingerprint. `GameRuntimeContext::navigation` is an additive
revocable host grant; `BehaviorContext::NavigationContext()` supplies the same
capability for native and Lua behaviors. The host binds one module/Scene and
allowed operation set, refreshes committed observations, revokes before reload,
and drains canonical results through `DrainRetired()` before retiring services.
Navigation never retains a module/VM callback. Scripts use opaque request values
with `poll()` and `cancel()`. Missing services return `navigation.capability_unavailable`.

Rollback: stop play, restore the coherent previous engine, SDK and rebuilt module
set, then restart. Never mix SDK9 and SDK10 module binaries.

## Gameplay SDK10 → SDK15 (Character and Navigation runtime boundary 10 → 15)

Rebuild every native gameplay module, descriptor bundle and host with the exact
`gameplay-sdk15` fingerprint. This layout preserves the merged SDK10 Navigation
context and appends the Character holder and behavior backend accessor. The
minimal GameplayApi target retains forward declarations only; native Character
consumers opt into the existing `Physics` SDK component.

Hosts supply inert anchors through `GameModuleHostOptions` and
`GameModuleRuntimeServices` (Physics, Character, Navigation). Legacy Physics and
Navigation constructors forward into the same composition. Each loaded module
and behavior runtime receives its independently revocable Character child.
Authoritative holders revoke before callbacks and release exposed copies before
native image retirement; omitted dependencies remain typed failures.

SDK15 also includes host-owned Physics batch and Prefab operation allocation and
immutable private issuer dispatch. These opaque cross-image contracts require a
new epoch even when public handle sizes do not change. SDK12/14 artifacts and
SDK10 artifacts must not be mixed with this combined layout. Parallel AI SDK16
and UI SDK17 layouts are independent; a future union requires a fresh epoch18+
and complete consumer/lifecycle revalidation, not the maximum prior number.

Rollback: restore a coherent previous engine, SDK and rebuilt module set, then
restart play. Never edit fingerprints or force-load a mismatched binary.

## Gameplay SDK19/20 → SDK21 (Navigation, Character, AI and Runtime UI union)

Rebuild the engine hosts, every native module, generated descriptor bundle and
all optional SDK consumers with boundary21 / `gameplay-sdk21`. This layout
preserves main SDK20's Physics, Navigation, AI and Character runtime context
fields, then appends Runtime UI. `GameRegistrationContext` retains main's inert
AI descriptor registry; the behavior backend retains its UI capability slot.
Host services preserve Physics, Character, Navigation and AI, then append UI.
Use designated fields rather than positional aggregates. SDK15/18/19/20 and all
earlier incompatible layouts are rejected before native factory activation.

AI diagnostics remain immutable host-owned snapshots. Authoritative AI task
retirement and UI scope revocation both precede native callbacks and image unload;
neither capability may replace the other's authority. Behavior factories retain
`BehaviorRuntimeServiceBindings` for Physics, Navigation, Character and UI, with
SceneService-only prefab admission separate. AI script composition retains its
existing explicit bridge; no service discovery or fallback is introduced.

SDK19 and SDK20 identify different published layouts; SDK21 is a fresh combined
reservation, never their numeric maximum. Rollback requires restoring a coherent
previous engine, SDK, rebuilt modules and manifests together. Never edit a
fingerprint to force-load an old binary. Historical migration sections below
describe their original layouts rather than the current combined boundary.

## Gameplay SDK15/17 → SDK19 (Character, Navigation and Runtime UI union)

Rebuild every native module, generated descriptor bundle, host and optional Physics
and RuntimeUi SDK consumer with boundary 19 and the exact `gameplay-sdk19`
fingerprint. SDK19 combines main Character SDK15 (including Navigation SDK10)
with Runtime UI SDK17. `GameRuntimeContext` preserves main Physics, Navigation,
Character order and appends `runtimeUi`; the behavior backend preserves Character
and appends UI before the existing Prefab capability. Runtime host services preserve
Physics, Character, Navigation and append UI. The runtime constructors accept
Character followed by UI, so UI callers must supply an empty Character argument
when no Character grant is admitted. Use designated host service fields.

Both capabilities retain separate host-derived scopes and revoke before teardown.
Minimal/headless consumers retain forward declarations; Physics and RuntimeUi
remain independent optional SDK components over one common exported support closure.
SDK10/15/17/18 binaries are rejected before factories. SDK18/20 AI layouts are
independent reservations and are not part of SDK19; a future UI+AI union needs
another fresh identity and complete consumer/lifecycle validation, never `max`.

Rollback: restore one coherent previous engine, SDK, descriptor/artifact manifests
and rebuilt module set, then restart play. Never edit fingerprints to load an old
binary into this combined layout.

## Gameplay SDK10 → SDK17 (Navigation and Runtime UI layout)

This combined layout preserves merged Navigation and appends `GameRuntimeContext::runtimeUi` and the matching
`BehaviorContext` backend virtual capability. It preserves existing field and
constructor ordering. Rebuild all native module descriptors, bundles and SDK
consumers with boundary 17 and the exact `gameplay-sdk17` fingerprint. The optional
`find_package(HoroEngineGameplay REQUIRED COMPONENTS RuntimeUi)` exposes canonical
Horo UI values/client calls; the default Gameplay API retains forward declarations
and does not import UI, renderer, input or platform implementations. Physics and
RuntimeUi share one exported common support closure and remain independent options.

Hosts compose `GameModuleHostOptions{capabilities, GameModuleRuntimeServices{physics,
runtimeUi, navigation}}`; legacy Physics constructors forward to this sole authority. A grant
is issued by the application-owned `RuntimeUiService`, then independently derived
for native module, behavior runner and each Lua VM. Revoke before callback admission
closes; module-facing context assignment cannot replace the authoritative holder.

SDK17 combines merged NAV SDK10 with Runtime UI. Parallel Character SDK15 and
AI SDK16 identify different layouts; a future union requires a fresh boundary18
or later, a new fingerprint and complete SDK revalidation. Taking a numeric
maximum is unsafe. Revalidate after main changes. Rollback requires rebuilding
the previous exact SDK and restoring its module/artifact manifest together;
never change a fingerprint to admit an old native binary.

## Gameplay SDK15 / AI18 → SDK20

This snapshot combines current-main Character15 and Navigation with the AI18
host-owned immutable diagnostic snapshot layout as boundary20 / `gameplay-sdk20`.
It excludes the separately reserved Runtime UI19 layout. SDK15, AI18 and UI19
modules and descriptor bundles are rejected before native factory activation.
Rebuild the engine hosts, every native module, descriptor bundles and public
consumers together; changing only a fingerprint never migrates a binary.

The canonical `GameModuleRuntimeServices` retains Physics, Character and Navigation
anchors and adds AI. Migrate earlier positional AI aggregates to designated `.ai`
fields. Legacy empty, Physics and Navigation host constructors retain their meaning.
Each module receives independently revocable AI and Character children; AI tasks
retire before module callbacks/image unload, while Navigation retains its exact
code-image pin and Physics/Character allocation authority remains host-owned.

Rollback: restore a coherent previous engine, SDK, rebuilt native modules and
matching descriptor/artifact manifests, then restart play. Never edit fingerprints
to admit a binary from another layout.

## External C ABI 1.4 → 1.5 settings and modal pages

ABI 1.5 appends `registerEditorPage` to `HoroExtensionHostApi`. It does not
change any 1.0–1.4 prefix, activity transport, or GameModule SDK21 layout.
Existing activity modules must continue to request the 1.4 prefix ending at
`offsetof(HoroExtensionHostApi, registerEditorPage)`, rather than recompiling an
old initializer with the new full `sizeof`. Rebuild page modules with the
packaged `EditorPageAbi.h`; query minor5, `HORO_EXTENSION_REQUIRES_EDITOR_PAGE`,
full host-table size and explicit `editor.page` capability. Load must repeat
size/version/null checks before dereferencing the new tail. Rollback removes
page claims/callbacks and uses the unchanged activity profile on an older host;
it never treats a settings/modal page as an activity or persistent document tab.

A proper explicit1.4 query receives only its original host prefix and no page callback. Page registration additionally requires the validated minor5/page-function/complete-prefix proof at ingress; manifest claims or a composed GUI alone cannot grant transport. The actual C11 legacy-query regression verifies that neither Settings nor Modal claims publish through the old negotiation.

Registration is staging only. The host copies definitions/messages and checks
manifest ownership, requested/approved permissions and exact capability grants
before transactional commit. Session `open`/`close` are owner-lane operations;
opening during module load fails. The host owns draft, focus, cancellation,
validation and active settings. Callback payloads and cancellation views are
borrowed for that call only. Native callbacks run on attributed jobs with code
and capability leases; revocation closes admission before draining callbacks.

Page schema1 supports bool, exact int64 and bounded single-line UTF-8 strings,
text/label/action and Stack/Group layout. Medium/Neutral styling and automatic
focus are the supported presentation profile. Row/Grid, path/choice, decimal,
color/vector, multiline and confirmation actions fail registration explicitly.
Integer fields use decimal text end-to-end, including signed endpoints and
values above2^53; converting them through double is invalid.

Settings pages choose one canonical User/Project/Workspace scope. The real GUI
composition supplies the existing durable filesystem and explicit selected
project/configuration roots. Apply validates the whole candidate and provider
findings, durably writes the canonical snapshot, then performs a noexcept active
swap. Any uncertain write retains dirty `RecoveryRequired` state and original
error until explicit reload. Modal fields are Session scoped, validate then
submit, and never persist. Open/draft/focus/modal stack data is never workspace
state. Reset/cancel do not write. Parent/child workflows require exact provider
and active parent identity; the existing modal host owns focus/input exclusivity.

Affected callers include `ExtensionManager`, native activation/retirement,
`EditorSurfaceRegistry`, `GuiScreenHost`, Settings/Modal host adapters, SDK
packaging/reconfigure/external C11 consumers and native page fixtures. All host
C++ consumers rebuild against the new additive controller contract. The GUI
adapter consumes validated `BuildEditorUiRenderSnapshot` from the actual shared
design tokens/fonts. A private owner-boundary structural capture generation
tracks real theme changes; it is neither persisted nor a second theme authority.

## External C ABI 1.5 → 1.6

`EditorInspectorAbi.h` describes copied inspector/property-drawer declarations.
Require ABI 1.6 and `HORO_EXTENSION_REQUIRES_EDITOR_INSPECTOR`
before using the appended registration callback. The 1.5 page prefix and 1.4
activity prefix retain their negotiated behavior. Declaration permission never
grants document ownership: the application must explicitly approve the current
provider for the exact behavior schema and fields before typed edits are admitted.

## External C ABI 1.6 → 1.7

Viewport providers use `EditorViewportAbi.h`, negotiate the complete appended
`registerEditorViewport` prefix and `HORO_EXTENSION_REQUIRES_EDITOR_VIEWPORT`,
and require interactive `editor.viewport` support. Existing field offsets remain
unchanged. See the repository guide `docs/guides/extension-viewport-migration.md`
for permissions, copied geometry budgets, canonical transform binding and teardown.
